## Follow the Design System layout

Use the same section order as the GOV.UK Design System. The information architecture follows [alphagov/govuk-design-system](https://github.com/alphagov/govuk-design-system): Get started, Styles, Components, Patterns, Community and Accessibility. Pages use a common header, service navigation, section navigation, main content and footer.

The upstream website uses Node, `src`, `views`, `data` and build tasks. It is not a Middleman project. This repository maps its layout and content organisation to Middleman's `source`, `layouts`, `data`, `helpers` and `config` directories. It does not claim to copy the upstream build stack.

## Local structure

```text
docs/
  Gemfile                 # Pinned govuk_tech_docs dependency
  Gemfile.lock            # Resolved dependency versions
  config.rb               # Official template setup and local exclusions
  config/tech-docs.yml    # Template configuration
  data/navigation.yml    # Shared page navigation
  source/                # Human-readable Markdown and assets
    layouts/             # Unbranded civica shell
    stylesheets/         # Selected GOV.UK/Tech Docs styles + local layout
    javascripts/         # Documentation demonstration behaviour
    design-contract.json # Machine-readable rules and scope
    llms.txt             # AI reading guide
  scripts/               # Palette and supplied-artwork synchronisation
```

## Keep the build unbranded

Do not publish official branding. Set `show_govuk_logo: false`, replace the shell and favicon paths, omit default font-face rules and import only the needed component styles. The published site excludes upstream branded images and font files. A dependency that contains official assets does not give civica permission to publish them. [Licensing](licensing.html) records the notices.

## Build and preview

Use Ruby 3.3 and Bundler. First, from the repository root, run `npm ci` and `npm run build` to build the shared chart examples. From `/docs`, run `bundle install`, then `bundle exec middleman build`. Use `bundle exec middleman server` for a local preview. Run the documentation validator after building.

Run `python3 docs/scripts/sync-brand.py --check` from the repository root to verify the accepted revised documentation artwork against `brand/civica/`.

Commit content, configuration and the lockfile. Do not commit `build/`, `vendor/` or `.bundle/`. Publish the generated `build/` directory through the repository's static hosting workflow.

The [Tech Docs Template](https://github.com/alphagov/tech-docs-template) explains its Middleman origin and its non-government branding requirements. [Sources](sources.html) owns the source record.

## Frontend startup

The global module waits for Home Assistant to define `home-assistant` before loading the colour settings and branding components. This keeps component registration in Home Assistant’s final custom-element registry, including older frontend versions that replace it during startup. The production-bundle regression simulates that replacement and duplicate module URLs. One bundled file is served; the readiness boundary does not add a network chunk.

The Energy presentation adapter uses Home Assistant's existing ECharts instance. The separate documentation/example bundle includes ECharts only to demonstrate that same adapter without connecting to a home. Build both from the shared source; do not maintain a different illustrative implementation in the docs.

## Shared examples and publication history

The [example viewer](../components/example.html) reads reviewed YAML records, creates isolated standalone pages and displays the exact rendered HTML. Safe demonstration forms start disabled and are enabled only after the local handler is installed; without JavaScript they cannot submit household-like values as URL queries. GOV.UK Frontend 6.6.0 supplies the standard component enhancement, while civica supplies the shell, safe demos and example tabs.

Give every non-home editorial page the [page-history pattern](../components/page-history.html). First-publication dates come from repository history, and updates are authored records. The build does not manufacture a fresh review date. Example canvases carry a demonstration notice and link from their owning guide, which holds the editorial history.

Navigation and search derive their pages from Middleman resources and frontmatter section/group values. Publishing pages appear within Components or Patterns instead of creating a competing top-level taxonomy. The JSON coverage record exposes the same catalogue for automated reading.

The homepage and category illustrations are original SVG compositions stored under `docs/source/images`. The supplied civica brand assets remain unchanged. All illustrations use ordinary interface shapes and avoid official government artwork.
